Print one entity's full field schema: types, operators, required, relations.

Every field entry carries the same 26 keys, using null or the sentinels "n/a" /
"unknown" where a fact could not be learned, so `jq '.data.fields[] |
select(.required)'` is total and never needs a presence check.

BLOCKS. A blocks field answers two different questions. WHICH blocks may go in it
is .blocks[FIELD] (slugs) and is always printed. WHAT IS INSIDE one is
--block <slug>, which prints that block type's own fields, their types, their
relationship targets and their required-ness with provenance.
--blocks-detail inlines every one of them at once and is OFF by default on
purpose. Measured on the live project: `pay describe pages` is 31 KB, and 70 KB
with --blocks-detail, while `--field layout` goes from 4.4 KB to 43 KB — ten
times the output of the most-run discovery command, to answer a question that
is asked one block at a time. .block_schemas_available names every block whose
schema is cached; --block <slug> prints the one you are about to write.

Required-ness inside a block is tri-state and is null more often than on a
collection field. Payload publishes NO input type for a block type, so §7.4's
NON_NULL trick has nothing to read; what is left is a NON_NULL on the block's
object type (proof of required) and the block's own config.ts on disk. A
plugin's blocks live in node_modules, which is never scanned, so their optional
fields stay "unknown" — never "not required".

SYNOPSIS
  pay describe <collection|global> [--field PATH] [--required-only] [--queryable] [--examples] [--sample]
  pay describe <collection|global> --block <blockType-slug> [--field PATH]
  pay describe <collection|global> [--field PATH] --blocks-detail

DISCOVERED IN THIS PROJECT
  profile=default base_url=- discovery_revision=-
  cache is cold — run 'pay discover' to populate

FLAGS
    --block BLOCK                describe ONE blockType's own field schema: what goes INSIDE a cta, not just that cta exists
    --blocks-detail              inline EVERY reachable blockType's field schema (off by default: see BLOCKS in `pay help describe`)
    --examples                   add generated, copy-pasteable commands for this entity
    --field FIELD                describe one field path in full (dotted, e.g. hero.links.link.url)
    -h, --help                   help for describe
    --queryable                  only fields usable in --where
    --required-only              only fields whose required is known true
    --sample                     also fetch one real document (one extra request)

  Root flags (every command): run 'pay --help' for the full list.
    --profile --base-url --output --path --quiet --verbose --timeout --deadline
    --no-cache --refresh --yes --dry-run --no-audit --no-redact --errors-to

OUTPUT
  data_kind: schema
  .data = {"kind":"collection","entity":{…},"fields":[{"path":"title","payload_type":"text","required":true,"queryable":true,"operators":["equals","contains",…]}],"required_paths":["title"]}
  Envelope: {"ok":true,"v":1,"command":"…","data_kind":"…","data":…,"meta":{…},"warnings":[]}

EXIT CODES
    0   success
    2   auth — missing, invalid, locked or unverifiable credential
    6   network — DNS, TLS, timeout or a 5xx from the server
    9   configuration — no base_url, unknown profile, secret in plaintext
    10  capability — this project does not support the operation
  
  Full table: pay explain --section exit_codes

EXAMPLES
  # everything about a collection
  pay describe pages

  # what must I supply to create one?
  pay describe pages --required-only --path .fields[].path

  # what can I filter on?
  pay describe pages --queryable --path .queryable_paths[]

  # one field in full, including its operators
  pay describe pages --field title

  # the blockTypes a blocks field accepts
  pay describe pages --field layout

  # what goes INSIDE one block type
  pay describe pages --block cta

  # just that block's required fields
  pay describe pages --block mediaBlock --path .required_fields[]

  # every block type of one field, in full
  pay describe pages --field layout --blocks-detail

  # schema plus a real document
  pay describe pages --sample

  # generated commands for this collection
  pay describe pages --examples --path .examples[]


COMMON MISTAKES
  1. Reading `required: null` as "not required".
     Instead: null means PayCLI never learned it. Attempt the write; a 400 names the missing fields precisely.
  2. Using a join field in --select.
     Instead: Join fields are read-only and are requested with --joins 'field:limit=5,sort=-createdAt'; .join_fields lists them.
  3. Assuming `sortable: true` was measured.
     Instead: Sortability is heuristic unless sortable_confidence says otherwise; Payload silently ignores an unsortable field.
  4. Expecting `blocks` to be filled on every project.
     Instead: Block slugs are not in the API; a null means no source resolved them. .blocks_source[FIELD] says which source did.
  5. Reusing one collection's block types for another field.
     Instead: Every blocks field has its own list: pages.layout and forms.fields share none. Read .blocks[FIELD].
  6. Writing a block with only the fields --block listed as required.
     Instead: Required-ness inside a block is often null (Payload publishes no input type for one). .block.required_unknown names every field nobody could answer for; .block.reason says why.
  7. Treating blockName or id as content.
     Instead: id, blockName and blockType are Payload plumbing. blockType is mandatory and must be the SLUG; id is server-generated; blockName is an optional admin label. .block.fields[].plumbing marks them.

SEE ALSO
  pay collections
  pay explain --collection <slug>
  pay find <collection> --select …

